PR #2419  overall: bug_confirmed
Spec: Obsolete spec NOTE described sign-extension when XLEN<SEW for Zfinx-family FP scalar operands, but RV32_Zdinx requires reading a register pair to supply EEW=64 scalars — implementations following the old text would corrupt 64-bit FP data
  [BUG] arch/riscv/kernel/cpufeature.c:175-196  riscv_ext_vector_float_validate
      The validator unconditionally requires CONFIG_FPU (line 181) and the D extension (line 192) for ALL vector float extensions. Zdinx+V systems are architecturally designed to operate without F/D registers—FP operands live in x-register pairs via Zdinx. This gate blocks legitimate Zdinx-capable hardware from enabling vector FP support. The check conflates 'needs floating-point capability' with 'needs F/D CSRs', which are distinct concerns under Zdinx.
  [BUG] arch/riscv/include/asm/hwcap.h:105-118  (ISA extension bitmap definitions)
      RISCV_ISA_EXT_ZFINX, RISCV_ISA_EXT_ZDINX, and RISCV_ISA_EXT_ZHINX are entirely absent from the ISA extension bitmap. The enumeration jumps from ZICFISS=105 to XLINUXENVCFG=127 with no Zdinx/Zfinx/Zhinx entries. Without these definitions, the kernel cannot detect Zdinx-capable hardware, cannot gate Zdinx-dependent behaviour (e.g. relaxing the D-extension requirement in riscv_ext_vector_float_validate), and cannot report Zdinx support to userspace via hwprobe. This is a concrete infrastructure gap that blocks all Zdinx enablement.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kernel/ptrace.c:131-159  invalid_ptrace_v_csr
      ELEN is derived solely from RISCV_ISA_EXT_ZVE64X presence (line 154), capping ELEN=32 on RV32 without ZVE64X, which then rejects SEW=64 vtype settings (lines 158-159). On RV32+Zdinx, vector FP instructions with EEW=64 and scalar operands sourced from x-register pairs are architecturally valid. The gate's assumption that 'ELEN=32 implies SEW=64 is always invalid' does not account for the Zdinx register-pair model where 64-bit scalar FP values are constructed from adjacent even/odd x-registers. Currently safe on all shipping hardware (no Zdinx silicon exists), but the invariant 'ZVE64X is the sole determinant of ELEN≥64' is not enforced in code and will break when Zdinx+V hardware appears.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kernel/vector.c:81-112  insn_is_vector
      insn_is_vector matches all V instructions via opcode=0x57 (RVV_OPCODE_VECTOR) without distinguishing integer from FP sub-types. On RV32+Zdinx, instructions like vfmacc.vf, vfmv.s.f, and vfredosum.vs have an architectural constraint requiring an even-numbered rs1/rd as the base of the x-register pair for the 64-bit scalar FP operand. The kernel does not validate this even-register constraint—a malformed encoding with an odd source register would pass through to hardware. If the kernel ever emulates these instructions (trap-and-emulate path), the emulation must account for the register-pair access pattern to avoid silently producing sign-extended-truncated 32-bit values instead of the full 64-bit pair. Currently this is a latent gap rather than an active bug since the kernel does not emulate these instructions and hardware enforces the constraint.
  [OK] arch/riscv/include/asm/vector.h:196-231  __riscv_v_vstate_save
      Vector state is saved bytewise with vse8.v (element-width-agnostic, line 220-225), so the save/restore path is correct regardless of the active SEW. On RV32+Zdinx, if a vfmacc.vf with EEW=64 were interrupted and rescheduled, the vtype CSR (including SEW=64) would be correctly saved and restored by __vstate_csr_save, and the scalar x-register pair holding the 64-bit FP operand is preserved by the normal pt_regs-based context switch. No Zdinx-specific code change is required here—the kernel implicitly relies on hardware to pair the registers, which is correct because the save path is Zdinx-unaware but also Zdinx-agnostic.

